<h1 class="article-title">Liepāja SEZ Authority Becomes a Full Member of WindEurope</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>The Liepāja Special Economic Zone Authority is taking part in the annual WindEurope offshore wind energy conference and exhibition in Bilbao, Spain, from 20 to 22 March, with particular involvement in the WindEurope ports working group. Liepāja has positioned itself as a leading offshore wind support base in the Baltic Sea region, as demonstrated by the 55-hectare land reservation agreement signed with Van Oord at the Port of Liepāja. On 18 March, the Liepāja SEZ Board decided to join the wind industry association WindEurope. The Authority is now a full member and will work actively alongside more than 500 European wind industry professionals.</p><p>Industry professionals have gathered for the annual WindEurope event to attend educational presentations and discussions highlighting the advantages of wind energy, learn about the latest industry developments and establish new partnerships. Latvia is represented for the first time at Europe’s leading annual wind industry conference and is among more than 500 exhibitors. The event’s motto is “Our Wind, Our Value – Creating Value for Europe, Living by European Values.”</p><p>The three-day conference also addresses the preservation of biodiversity and the need to accelerate the construction of electricity grids and port infrastructure, which are essential both during offshore wind farm construction and for subsequent servicing and maintenance.</p><p>On 21 March, Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis shared Liepāja’s chosen development direction at the Mission Latvia stand of the Investment and Development Agency of Latvia (LIAA), explaining its strengths as a prospective leading offshore wind support base in the Baltic Sea. “First, our geographical location is highly advantageous: all planned Baltic Sea wind farms are within a 400 km radius. Second, the inner waters of the Port of Liepāja include a development area suitable for an offshore wind support base under the city and Liepāja SEZ development plans. Third, we have both educational capacity to train specialists and industrial capacity to manufacture a range of components. News of the Liepāja SEZ Authority’s plans with Van Oord has generated considerable interest, particularly among potential investors. This means we need to explain our plans, answer questions and arrange further meetings and visits to Liepāja,” emphasised Uldis Hmieļevskis.</p><p>The three-day exhibition and conference brings together more than 12,000 participants, 520 companies, 14 ministers and 400 other speakers from the wind industry, politics and academia. Discussions cover the value of wind power, jobs, nature conservation, energy security, economic competitiveness, participation by local communities and other issues.</p><p>Investment in new wind farms, including those in the Baltic Sea, has grown rapidly in recent years. Ports and their infrastructure are essential support bases for constructing, servicing and maintaining those wind farms.</p><p>Wind farms currently generate 19% of all electricity consumed in Europe. The EU aims to increase this figure to 35% by 2030, strengthening European energy security and independence. This means almost doubling wind power capacity over the next seven years. The latest WindEurope association report: https://windeurope.org/newsroom/press-releases/uptake-in-permitting-and-investments-brings-2030-wind-target-within-reach/</p><p> </p></div>
